# Cache-Control rules. This deploys as a static-assets Worker, not Pages,
# but the _headers file works the same way. It lives in public/ so
# `vite build` copies it verbatim into dist/, the directory wrangler serves.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/headers/
#
# HTML pages stay short-lived so edits (new cases, copy fixes) show up quickly.
#
# Everything under /assets/ is cached for a year and marked immutable, which
# is safe because Vite content-hashes every filename it emits there
# (game-engine-D41xyz.js): changed bytes mean a changed URL, by construction.
# The hashed URL lives in the HTML, which expires in 300s, so every visitor
# picks up an asset change within five minutes of a deploy. This used to rely
# on a hand-bumped ?v=N token on every asset URL, enforced by a CI script;
# the bundler replaced both.
#
# Without per-version URLs this was a real bug, not just staleness: HTML
# expires after 300s but assets sat in the browser cache for 24h, so a
# returning visitor could pair NEW html with an OLD game-engine.js. Add a
# question type and the stale engine renders the prompt with no answer UI at
# all (buildInteractiveBody has no else branch); add an engine helper and the
# page throws and #app stays blank. Both are silent — no error, nothing in
# the console.
#
# One exception to the hashing: /assets/img/og-image.jpg keeps a stable,
# unhashed URL (it lives in public/, which the build copies as-is) because
# the og:image and twitter:image URLs are consumed by social scrapers, which
# cache by URL and ignore Cache-Control entirely. Replacing the share card
# means a new filename, updated in both meta tags in index.html — nothing
# else picks it up. It still matches the /assets/* rule below; immutable is
# fine for a file that only ever changes by renaming.
#
# nosniff and Referrer-Policy are free and worth having. A full CSP is not:
# the homepage and every game page rely on inline <script> and <style> (a
# deliberate render-blocking fix, see 42edcb2), so a CSP would need
# 'unsafe-inline' for scripts and lose most of its value. There is also no
# injection path to defend — no user input is stored or reflected, and every
# innerHTML interpolation takes generated numbers or page-authored literals.

# There is deliberately no /index.html rule: html_handling redirects that URL
# to / before headers apply, so it could never match. Nothing links to it
# either — every internal link on the site is written extension-less.
/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, must-revalidate
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

/games/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, must-revalidate
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
